#{[17592186311754 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311755 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186311756 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)}{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314783 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4}=\\frac{2}{5},\n$$  what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314786 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314788 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314916 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314917 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314918 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186314992 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317512 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}tan\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186320874 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/a4/a4961fa6f4f1aafeba2e8f77ed91e7d034fffd6aa249f13bb8357da11a7cea5e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot (0-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320875 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/24/24ca4823d220c3eb31127ed28ab7c75b1297e10ae20a1926d112a234806c411e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320876 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)]}_{a}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186320904 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/7151efaca7e4356e590a437f5d50a2ab6e5646e960b7ffa82b0bbed27bb9db47.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323732 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\n$$"] [17592186335841 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi }{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$"] [17592186339091 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)}=\n$$"] [17592186339092 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.\n$$"] [17592186339093 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)}=\n$$"] [17592186342011 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }.\n$$"] [17592186365797 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186371000 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $u(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ and \n\n$$\nu(0)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(u)$ is continuous at $u=1$ and $u(t)$ is continuous at $t=0,$ the function $f(u(t))$ is also continuous at $t=0.$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to 0}f(u(t))=f(u(0))=f(1).\n$$"] [17592186376552 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε>0,$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $ε.$\n\nFirst, simplifying $|{a}_{n}-L|,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}+\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3(1-{n}^{2})}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}+\\frac{3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3-3{n}^{2}+3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}>0$ for any natural $n\\ge 2.$ With that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n|{a}_{n}-L|=|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|=\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $|{a}_{n}-L|<ε$ for $n\\ge 2:$\n\nSolving this inequality for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} & <ε \\\\ 4 & <ε(9{n}^{2}-21) \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε} & <9{n}^{2}-21 \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε}+21 & <9{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $$\n\\displaystyle |\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε,\n$$ it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\nTherefore, we choose $N>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\frac{4}{9ε}>0,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}≈1.53\n$$ and therefore $N\\ge 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\nThen, for any natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| & =\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} \\\\  & <\\frac{4}{9{(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}})}^{2}-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(21+\\frac{4}{ε}-21)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186504629 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right))=\n$$"] [17592186504644 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186504684 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186504689 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\n$$"] [17592186507452 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=\n$${{field-1}}"] [17592186563667 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & =\\lim_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563693 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563712 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563716 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563754 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186565477 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\n$$"] [17592186573250 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+\\pi )=\n$$"] [17592186573309 :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right)?\n$$"] [17592186573309 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right):$ \n\n![](images/ff/fffe2ba1124b8edd04902db5ad2b0945e5e04372ae0a0ed338c893f0ae417c58.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right)$ is $x\\in [2,6].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right)\n$$ is undefined."] [17592186580285 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$"] [17592186580285 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & ={\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{g\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x))}^{′}}{(g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{{g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x))}^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{{g}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1-4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186582369 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /4)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}.\n$$"] [17592186582389 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$"] [17592186583003 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $u(t)=\\sqrt{3t+7}$ is continuous at $t=-1$ and \n\n$$\nu(-1)=\\sqrt{3(-1)+7}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(u)$ is continuous at $u=2$ and $u(t)$ is continuous at $t=-1,$ the function $f(u(t))$ is also continuous at $t=-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to -1}f(u(t))=f(u(-1))=f(2).\n$$"] [17592186583014 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $u(t)={t}^{2}+1$ is continuous at $t=2$ and \n\n$$\nu(2)={2}^{2}+1=5.\n$$\n\nSince $f(u)$ is continuous at $u=5$ and $u(t)$ is continuous at $t=2,$ the function $f(u(t))$ is also continuous at $t=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to 2}f(u(t))=f(u(2))=f(5).\n$$"] [17592186596625 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)}=\n$${{field-1}}."] [17592186596625 :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})}{\\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that $\\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,f(x)=f\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,{f}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})=0+{8}^{1}=8, \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))=\\frac{4}{5}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡0=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})}{\\lim_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186611630 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)}{2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186616971 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{36+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{36+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{6}\\right)]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{6}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{6}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/fa/fa90489ad28c3fde493a83b61ef040867d575ad06c989439de9764d0d23b002e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{36+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{6}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186617030 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/31/319d7a909eb214a44690c374ab83fc5b46c531018d82583bc7b8782e2283b13f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186629301 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{5}\\right)]}_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f2/f2ffb5a9f3439f2ff83ce24e740e762eb34eaa4e52bc9fa244084490e730e159.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
